5. RED EVIDENCE — ORDERING PROVED BY TREE HASH, NOT BY CLOCK
================================================================================
Reds for behaviours 1, 3 and 4 are committed INSIDE their fix commits. Timestamp
ordering alone would be weak: `bisellium red` accepts `--now <iso>`, so `at` is a
flag, not a witness. But each log's `# tree:` header is NOT a clock — runRed calls
sourceTreeHash(repo, excludes, "HEAD") (packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:576-583),
a sha1 over `git ls-tree -r HEAD` with the officina excluded. I recomputed that hash
for every commit on the branch and matched it against each log:

  log     recorded tree           = HEAD at          its fix commit    verdict
  01.log  dirty:8d44a97a...       6db2011            c14a486           red ran on the PARENT
  03.log  dirty:0074278c...       c14a486            a9cec97           red ran on the PARENT
  04.log  dirty:d1c6eb72...       a9cec97            fc85565           red ran on the PARENT
  02.log  dirty:aba8ab50...       fc85565            718ae31           red ran on the PARENT

Every red's own header names, cryptographically, the commit immediately BEFORE the
commit that fixed it, and every one is marked `dirty` (the uncommitted test file).
This is verifiable by DESIGN, not by luck — materially different from the W-046
stash case, where nothing in the artifact fixed the tree it ran against. Combined
with the log bodies, which betray the pre-fix implementation in their own text
("postWrite: not implemented" x8 in 03.log; "has a web-e2e job" FAIL in 04.log;
403 on both tuple 200 cases in 01.log), red-first holds in substance here.

Distinct bodies, non-zero exits, all four present: yes.

