F-1  ADVISORY — the bundle-grep test cannot catch the mutation the brief assigns it.
     apps/web/tests-serve/auth.spec.ts:90 "the built dist carries no copy of the live
     write-auth token" greps the dist for `served.token`. That token is
     randomBytes(24).toString("hex"), freshly generated at every serve start
     (packages/cli/src/serve.ts:117), with no env override. A value baked into the
     bundle at BUILD time can therefore never equal it, so the test passes
     unconditionally against the regression it is named for.
     DEMONSTRATED, not argued: I restored the import.meta.env fallback in api.ts and
     rebuilt with VITE_BISELLIUM_TOKEN=baked-secret-abc123. The secret DID land in
     apps/web/dist/assets/index-CykhaL5u.js — and that very test still passed. What
     actually caught M6 were three OTHER specs failing, because a baked token makes
     hasToken() true so the prompt never opens.
     So the suite bites (the gate is sound) but the brief's attribution is wrong and
     the test is near-vacuous for its stated purpose. A test that would bite:
     assert the dist contains no long hex run matching /[0-9a-f]{48}/, or grep for
     the literal string "VITE_BISELLIUM_TOKEN" in the built assets.
     ONLY INSTANCE of its kind here: I checked the other three behaviour-2 specs and
     all four behaviour-4 workflow assertions; each was independently proved to bite
     by M7/M10a/M10b/M11 above.

F-2  ADVISORY — a false statement about its own mechanism, inside W-047's signed census.
     packages/cli/src/containment.test.ts:250-251 justifies the new 30th entry with
     "the census is source-wide by construction (no test-file exclusion for inventory
     B, unlike inventory A's non-test filter)". There is no such asymmetry. Both
     inventories run over the same sourceFiles(), which applies one shared filter,
     isExcludedRel (:54-59). The real reason answer.spec.ts was pulled in is narrower
     and more useful: line 58 excludes "*.test.ts" but NOT "*.spec.ts", so Playwright
     specs sit inside the census source set while unit tests do not.
     Consequence: every future .spec.ts that joins an id onto a ".md" path will keep
     being forced into a hand-signed security census. Either exclude ".spec.ts"
     alongside ".test.ts", or say plainly that spec files are in scope — but the
     comment as written will mislead the next builder.
     SEARCHED: read isExcludedRel and sourceFiles in full, and enumerated the census
     source set at runtime (94 files) to confirm .spec.ts files are present and
     .test.ts files are not. ONLY INSTANCE — no other comment in this file makes a
     mechanism claim about the filter.

F-3  ADVISORY, PRE-EXISTING, NOT W-067's — latent cross-test fixture poisoning.
     packages/cli/src/writes.test.ts:31 does cpSync(examples/sample-studio, tmp,
     {recursive:true}), which copies the gitignored .bisellium/ directory along with
     the officina. Lines :203 and :230 then assert "exactly one event on disk". Any
     `bisellium serve --studio examples/sample-studio` run against the IN-TREE fixture
     seeds .bisellium/events.jsonl and permanently breaks `npm test` until someone
     deletes that untracked file by hand. This is what fired during pre-review triage.
     It predates W-067 and W-067 does not make it worse: this opus's own harness
     (apps/web/tests-serve/harness.ts:56-57) copies the fixture TO a temp dir and
     serves THAT, never the fixture itself — I confirmed after my own live probe run
     that examples/sample-studio/.bisellium/events.jsonl still does not exist.
     Recommend filing as its own opus: have freshStudio() skip .bisellium, or have
     the emit assertions count only events they wrote.
     SEARCHED for the class: grepped every cpSync/cp -r of sample-studio across
     packages/, apps/ and scripts/. Two sites — writes.test.ts:31 (vulnerable, asserts
     an absolute event count) and tests-serve/harness.ts:56 (not vulnerable, asserts
     nothing about pre-existing events). No third.

