  (F-1 notes the irony that the surviving sub-test is the weak one — but that is
  orthogonal to the disclosure's honesty.)

BUILDER DISCLOSURE 2 (reds bundled into fix commits): SATISFIED IN SUBSTANCE HERE —
  see §5. The `tree:` header, not the timestamp, is what carries it, and it is a
  content hash written by the tool. I am explicitly NOT ruling that timestamp
  ordering suffices; it does not, and I would not have passed on `at` alone.
  ON WHETHER THIS NEEDS A CHECK RULE: YES, AND I AM RAISING IT AS A PETITIO RATHER
  THAN ADOPTING IT. This is now the third case-by-case ruling on red ordering
  (W-046 round 3, this opus's disclosures 1 and 2). The studio's own standing rule
  is that process enforcement is a check rule or a hook, never a memory or prose —
  three rulings in prose is exactly the shape that rule forbids. The rule I would
  propose is NOT "reds must be in separate commits" (a commit is not evidence), but
  the stronger and already-available one: a red log's `tree:` hash must equal the
  source-tree hash of an ANCESTOR of the commit that introduces it. That is
  mechanically checkable from what `bisellium red` already records, it would have
  passed this opus without argument, and it would have caught a backdated red that
  a separate-commit rule would wave through. Filed for the retro; a blocking rule
  is the Patron's to grant, not mine (qa lex §4).

BUILDER DISCLOSURE 3 (the brief self-contradiction): RESOLVED CORRECTLY, AND
  DISCLOSING IT IS WHAT MAKES IT ACCEPTABLE. The brief's Out-of-scope says the token
  UI is "inside W-024's Inbox"; the Token-delivery section mandates an App-level,
  screen-agnostic prompt twice, argues for it explicitly ("An Inbox-local prompt
  would satisfy this opus's own behaviours while making W-065's mounted 401 case
  unbuildable; that is the seam, and this is which side it falls on"), and the
  Files-owned block lists App.tsx and a new components/TokenPrompt.tsx while saying
  the Inbox change is "the failure message only ... adds no token UI here". The
  detailed section is specific, reasoned and thrice-stated; the Out-of-scope line is
  a one-clause summary that did not get updated when the opus was carved out of
  W-065. Following the detailed section is right, and the implementation matches it
  exactly (App.tsx:46 mounts it once, Inbox.tsx carries no token UI).
  This is the class W-047's F-1 punished — but W-047 punished SILENCE, not judgment.
  Disclosed, argued, and matching the brief's own stated reasoning is the correct
  handling of a brief conflict. NOT A FINDING.
  The brief's Out-of-scope bullet should be corrected on merge so the next reader of
  the spec of record is not misled.

BUILDER DISCLOSURE 4 (.env.example deleted): CORRECT AND REQUIRED. The brief mandates
  removing the import.meta.env fallback; leaving a file whose entire content is
  "VITE_BISELLIUM_TOKEN=<token>" would document a path that no longer exists and
  contradict D-023's PATRON-5 amendment. Deletion is the smaller and more honest
